course: dictionary
lesson: 14939
title: Rules and regulations
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A regulation is an official rule. Governments and organisations make regulations to keep people safe and fair. «Safety regulations say you must wear a seatbelt in a car.» Here, regulation means an official rule that everyone has to follow.
explain: Regulation can also mean controlling something with rules. «The regulation of traffic keeps cars and bikes moving safely.» This is about the act of controlling, not one single rule. Ask yourself: is the word naming a rule, or the job of making things follow rules?
explain: As an adjective, regulation means standard or official. «The referee used a regulation football, the right size for the game.» If you can swap in "the official, standard kind" and the sentence still makes sense, regulation is being used as an adjective.
fact: A regulation is an official rule made by a government or organisation.
fact: Phrases you may meet include «safety regulations», «school regulations» and «rules and regulations».
fact: «Regulation» can mean controlling something with rules, as in «the regulation of traffic».
fact: In science, your body uses regulation to keep its temperature steady.
fact: As an adjective, «regulation» means standard and official, such as «regulation size».
exercise: «Wearing a helmet on a bike is one of the school's safety regulations.» What is a regulation here? | A kind of sport | A type of bicycle | A loud noise | *An official rule
exercise: «The referee checked that the pitch was regulation size.» What does regulation mean in this sentence? | *Standard and official | Broken and muddy | Very large and bouncy | Painted green
exercise: «The regulation of traffic keeps people safe on busy roads.» Which word tells you that regulation is about controlling something with rules? | people | roads | *traffic | safe
test: «Every shop must follow the health regulations about keeping food cold.» What does regulations mean in this sentence? | *Official rules that must be followed | Places where food is sold, like a market | Kinds of cold weather | Pieces of food
test: Mia says, «Regulation football is the same as a normal football.» What is wrong with Mia's idea? | Regulation means a very small ball for playing in the garden | *Regulation means the official, standard kind, so it is the correct size and rules for the game | Regulation means a ball that is painted red | Nothing is wrong with it
exam: Ella asks, «Is regulation a noun or an adjective?» Which answer is correct? | *It can be both: a noun meaning an official rule, or an adjective meaning standard | Only an adjective, and it never names a rule | It is a name for a country | Only a verb, which means to run
